INT. VICTORIAN TELEGRAPH OFFICE - NIGHT

Gaslight hisses. Brass keys. A wall of pigeonholes and paper tape curling like ivy. The air smells of lamp oil and wet wool. A grandfather clock ticks toward midnight.

ELIZABETH HARGROVE-WYNNE, 30s, in a gray wool dress with high collar, sits at the main key, fingers dancing. A pince-nez is perched on her nose. Her supervisor, MR. THADDEUS OSGOOD, 60s, a bald man with muttonchops, hovers over her shoulder. A messenger boy, PERCIVAL "PERCY" TWEEDLE, 12, dozes on a stool. A cleaner, MRS. KEZIAH AMADI, 50s, mops the floor with deliberate slowness. On a bench, CAPTAIN RUPERT BELLAMY, 40s, a naval officer with a bandaged hand, waits with his cap on his knee.

The key clatters. Elizabeth jots a line. She stops, rereads it, and slides it to Osgood.

ELIZABETH
Mr. Osgood. This came from Calais.

OSGOOD
(reading)
Good Lord.

CAPTAIN BELLAMY
What is it?

OSGOOD
Nothing, sir. A routine dispatch.

ELIZABETH
It is not routine.

OSGOOD
Miss Hargrove-Wynne.

ELIZABETH
It names the Admiralty. It says the Channel fleet has been sold.

Percy wakes, rubs his eyes. Mrs. Amadi stops mopping. Bellamy rises, face pale.

CAPTAIN BELLAMY
Sold? To whom?

ELIZABETH
It does not say.

OSGOOD
It is a forgery.

ELIZABETH
It came on the Admiralty cipher.

OSGOOD
Then it is a very good forgery.

The door bangs open. A tall man in evening dress enters, shaking rain from his coat: SIR ALARIC FENWICK-ROTH, 50s, an MP, with silver hair and a gold-topped cane. He sweeps off his hat.

SIR ALARIC
Mr. Osgood. I understand there has been a message.

OSGOOD
Sir Alaric. We were just discussing it.

SIR ALARIC
Then let us discuss it privately.

He extends a gloved hand. Osgood hesitates, glances at Elizabeth, then slowly hands over the slip. Sir Alaric reads, folds it, and tucks it inside his coat.

SIR ALARIC (CONT'D)
Miss, you have not seen this.

ELIZABETH
I have seen it, sir. I transcribed it.

SIR ALARIC
Then you will un-transcribe it.

Percy sneaks a look at Mrs. Amadi. She raises one eyebrow. Bellamy puts on his cap and steps forward.

CAPTAIN BELLAMY
Sir Alaric, I would like to see that dispatch.

SIR ALARIC
Captain, I am afraid it concerns matters above your station.

CAPTAIN BELLAMY
My ship is in that fleet.

A beat. Sir Alaric studies him, then smiles thinly and removes the slip.

SIR ALARIC
So it is.

He hands it over. Bellamy reads. His jaw tightens. He looks at Elizabeth.

CAPTAIN BELLAMY
Miss Hargrove-Wynne. I should like to send a reply.

ELIZABETH
To Calais?

CAPTAIN BELLAMY
To everyone.

She turns to the key. The clock begins to strike midnight.
